  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Saturday/... \nBy Wednesday, deep troughing and strong negative height anomalies \nwill be established off the central/southern California coast, and \npoised to move inland the remainder of the week. Ensemble and \ndeterministic modeling have come into much better agreement over \nthe past 24 hours with EPS members largely abandoning the idea of \na deeper, slower troughing driving eastward through the CWA. \nInstead, the preponderance of ensemble output now support previous\nGEFS solutions showing shortwave energy and the main cold core \nlifting from southern California into southern Utah in response \nto anomalous high amplitude, strong ridging stationed over the \ncentral/southern plains. However, ECMWF forecasts continue to \nhighlight more robust moisture advection via deep, meridional flow\nahead of the trough passage into Arizona, though differences \namong ensemble members are now becoming much smaller. \n\nRegardless, Wednesday and Thursday continue to have the best window \nfor rainfall potential with the synoptic pattern strongly favoring \npersistent moist upslope and orographically focused moderate \nrainfall north and east of Phoenix. Occasional bands of showers will \nundoubtedly extend into lower desert communities of south-central\nArizona with much of SE California/SW Arizona remaining dry. The \nadjusted northerly track lifting away from the CWA also decreases \nthe potential for strong thunderstorm development, though a few \nembedded storms would still be likely before rapid drying behind a\nfrontal passage sweeps through the region Friday. With better \nensemble agreement, the full suite of the NBM temperature spread \nhas narrowed slightly during this time frame with the expectation \nof additional forecast confidence in future iterations showing a \nrapid descent towards readings around 10F below normal by the end \nof the week.",
